Subject: kms: do not read a closed response body, and keep the observed status

Carried downstream while arnika-project/arnika#44 and #49 are OPEN. The two
upstream PRs squashed into one patch, because this repository needs the end
state and cannot apply a stack.

  #44  a non-OK status on the final attempt left a CLOSED body in `res`, so
       io.ReadAll failed with "http: read on closed response body". And a
       4xx/5xx is a successful HTTP transaction, so err was nil and nothing
       distinguished "the KMS refused" from a real fetch.

  #49  clearing `res` also discarded the STATUS, so the exhausted-loop error
       read the same for 503 (key pool momentarily empty, self-heals) and
       401/403/404 (cert or SAE user rejected, will never rotate again). The
       second group means the tunnel is coasting on a stale PSK with no fresh
       QKD material coming -- which arnika's own SECURITY.md asks operators to
       watch for, with nothing to alert on. Requested in review on #44 by the
       maintainer.

Constraints held, both from that review: status code only, never the response
body; and no request path in the message, because dec_keys carries key_ID in
its query string.

`ErrKMSUnavailable` is a sentinel so callers can use errors.Is rather than
match wording. New for the module: `errors` was not imported anywhere, and
while `%w` appears in about twenty places nothing calls errors.Is on the
result. Upstream may yet ask for a plain message instead -- if #49 merges in a
different shape, this patch and the Dockerfile assertions move with it.

LIFECYCLE. Delete this file when both merge and the pin moves. Keep the
`grep -q` in nodes/alice/Dockerfile and nodes/strongswan/Dockerfile, and make
sure the string it looks for matches whatever upstream actually merged -- a
grep left behind searching for a string that no longer exists passes nothing
and protects nothing. The assertion targets the SENTINEL NAME rather than the
message text for that reason: wording can change, the identifier is load
bearing.

The upstream tests are not carried; this repository asserts the property from
its own build instead.

diff --git a/repositories/kms.go b/repositories/kms.go
index ac74910..ee90e24 100644
--- a/repositories/kms.go
+++ b/repositories/kms.go
@@ -6,6 +6,7 @@ import (
 	"crypto/x509"
 	"encoding/base64"
 	"encoding/json"
+	"errors"
 	"fmt"
 	"io"
 	"log"
@@ -102,27 +103,67 @@ func (r *HTTPKMSRepository) GetKeyByID(keyID *string) (key []byte, err error) {
 	return key, err
 }
 
+var ErrKMSUnavailable = errors.New("KMS did not deliver a key")
+
 func (r *HTTPKMSRepository) kmsRequest(path string) (id string, key []byte, err error) {
 	var kmsResp kmsResponse
 	var res *http.Response
+	// The last HTTP status actually observed. Clearing res below is what makes
+	// the post-loop nil-check work, but it also discards the status, and the
+	// three cases that reach here need different responses from an operator:
+	// 503 is a momentarily empty key pool that the next interval recovers from,
+	// while 401, 403 and 404 mean the tunnel is coasting on a stale PSK with no
+	// fresh QKD material coming. Reporting them identically leaves nothing to
+	// alert on. Zero means no HTTP response was ever received.
+	var lastStatus int
 
 	for attempt := 0; attempt <= r.maxRetries; attempt++ {
 		res, err = r.conn.Get(r.baseURL + path)
 		if err == nil && res.StatusCode == http.StatusOK {
 			break
 		}
+		// Close and clear before deciding whether to retry, so that a body is
+		// never carried out of the loop after being closed. Previously the
+		// close happened after the retry decision and res kept its value, so
+		// a non-OK status on the final attempt left a closed body in res --
+		// and because a 4xx/5xx is a successful HTTP transaction, err was nil
+		// and the check below did not return. io.ReadAll then failed with
+		// "http: read on closed response body".
+		if res != nil {
+			lastStatus = res.StatusCode
+			_ = res.Body.Close()
+			res = nil
+		}
 		if attempt < r.maxRetries {
 			delay := r.backoffBaseDelay * time.Duration(1<<uint(attempt))
-			log.Printf("Attempt %d: Retrying in %s...", attempt+1, delay)
+			// The status goes in the per-attempt line as well as the final
+			// error, because this one fires every time rather than only at
+			// exhaustion. Status code only: the response body is never logged,
+			// and neither is the path -- dec_keys carries key_ID in its query
+			// string.
+			if lastStatus != 0 {
+				log.Printf("Attempt %d: KMS returned %d, retrying in %s...",
+					attempt+1, lastStatus, delay)
+			} else {
+				log.Printf("Attempt %d: Retrying in %s...", attempt+1, delay)
+			}
 			time.Sleep(delay)
 		}
-		if res != nil {
-			_ = res.Body.Close()
-		}
 	}
 	if err != nil {
 		return "", nil, err
 	}
+	// A retry loop that never saw a 200 leaves res nil. Without this the
+	// caller could not distinguish "the KMS refused" from a successful fetch,
+	// because a non-OK status sets no error of its own.
+	//
+	// lastStatus is always set here. A transport error on the final attempt
+	// returns above, so reaching this line means at least one attempt received
+	// an HTTP response -- there is no path that reports "status 0".
+	if res == nil {
+		return "", nil, fmt.Errorf("%w: status %d after %d attempt(s)",
+			ErrKMSUnavailable, lastStatus, r.maxRetries+1)
+	}
 	defer func() { _ = res.Body.Close() }()
 
 	body, err := io.ReadAll(res.Body)
